course: dictionary
lesson: 13911
title: Launder: clean clothes or dirty money
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have two meanings, and the context tells you which one you need. «The hotel laundered the guests' towels every day.» Towels get washed and ironed in a hotel, so launder means to wash and iron clothes or sheets. This is a formal word. At home, you would say wash or do the laundry.
explain: Now read this: «He laundered the stolen money through a car wash so it looked honest.» Stolen money is not honest, and the words so it looked honest tell you he was hiding where it came from. In crime stories and the news, launder means to hide dishonestly gained money by passing it through a business or bank. This is called money laundering, and it is a crime.
explain: Always test your meaning. Put "wash" into the towel sentence: the hotel washed the towels every day, which makes sense. Put "hide where the money came from" into the money sentence, and it makes sense too. If your meaning fits the clues around the word, you are probably right.
fact: Launder is a formal word, so it is often used in hotels, schools and official writing.
fact: «Launder» means to wash and iron clothes, sheets or towels.
fact: «Launder money» means to hide where dishonestly gained money came from by putting it through a business or bank so it looks honest.
fact: Money laundering is a crime, and the news often reports it.
fact: The everyday words for the clothes meaning are wash and do the laundry; launder is just the more formal choice.
exercise: «The hotel laundered the guests' towels every day.» What does laundered mean here? | Folded and put in a drawer | Thrown away | *Washed and ironed | Hidden in a bank
exercise: «He laundered the stolen money through a car wash so it looked honest.» What does laundered mean here? | Counted it carefully | Gave it to charity | *Hid where the money came from | Spent it all on cars
exercise: Mum says she will do the laundry. Which everyday word means the same as launder when it is about clothes? | Lock | Paint | Bury | *Wash
test: «Mum laundered my school shirt before the match.» What does laundered mean here? | Dropped | Hidden | *Washed and ironed | Torn
test: «Money laundering is a serious crime.» What kind of crime is it? | *Hiding dishonestly gained money so it looks honest | Shouting in a library | Stealing bikes | Breaking a window
exam: «The news said the gang had tried to launder millions through a chain of shops.» What does launder mean here? | Count the coins one by one | Spend it on holidays | *Hide where the dirty money came from | Wash the shop floors
